# From Chaos to Zeus

Hesiod's *Theogony*, composed around 700 BCE, tells how the world and its
gods came to be. First Chaos comes into being, then Gaia, Tartarus and
Eros. Gaia bears Uranus, the sky, and with him the Titans. Kronos, the
youngest Titan, castrates Uranus and rules until his own son Zeus
overthrows him. The order of the [[olympians]] is the end of that
succession.

```hql id=names hidden
import myth.kb

to_map(kb.beings(), b => b.id, b => b.name)
```

## The births, in the poem's order

Every birth the *Theogony* tells, each set of parents once with the
children the passage gives them. One parent alone means a birth without
union: Gaia bears Uranus and Pontus by herself, and Hera bears Hephaestus.

```hql
import myth.kb

fn opening(passage: String) : Int =
    passage.split(",")[0].unwrap_or("").split("–")[0].unwrap_or("") | to_int | unwrap_or(0)

type Births { lines : String, parents : String, children : String }

name = (id: String) => names.get(id).unwrap_or(id)

kb.parentage()
| filter(a => a.work == "theogony")
| group(by = a => fmt("{} {}", a.passage, a.parents | join(" ")))
| map(g => Births(
    lines    = g.items[0].map(a => a.passage).unwrap_or(""),
    parents  = g.items[0].map(a => a.parents).unwrap_or([]) | map(name) | join(" and "),
    children = g.items | map(a => name(a.child)) | join(", "),
  ))
| sort(by = b => opening(b.lines))
| Table(columns = ["lines", "parents", "children"])
```
