## The succession

The myths of the succession, in the order they happen, and where each is
told.

```hql
import myth.kb
import myth.stories

works = kb.works()

kb.myths()
| filter(m => m.seq <= 9)
| map(m => {myth: m.title, told: m.sources(works) | join("; ")})
| Table(columns = ["myth", "told"])
```
<!-- hql:output hash=190d4dacacc8 status=ok -->
| myth                             | told                                                               |
| -------------------------------- | ------------------------------------------------------------------ |
| The first beings                 | Hesiod, Theogony 116–125                                           |
| Gaia bears Uranus and the Titans | Hesiod, Theogony 126–153                                           |
| Kronos castrates Uranus          | Hesiod, Theogony 154–206; Pseudo-Apollodorus, Library 1.1.4        |
| Kronos swallows his children     | Hesiod, Theogony 453–491; Pseudo-Apollodorus, Library 1.1.5–1.1.7  |
| Zeus frees his siblings          | Hesiod, Theogony 492–506; Pausanias, Description of Greece 10.24.6 |
| The war of the Titans            | Hesiod, Theogony 617–720; Pseudo-Apollodorus, Library 1.2.1        |
| The world divided by lot         | Homer, Iliad 15.187–193                                            |
| Typhon                           | Hesiod, Theogony 820–880; Pseudo-Apollodorus, Library 1.6.3        |
| The war of the Giants            | Pseudo-Apollodorus, Library 1.6.1–1.6.2                            |
<!-- /hql:output -->

## Where the sources disagree

The same being, born of different parents in different works. Hesiod even
disagrees with himself: the Moirai are daughters of Night at one point of
the poem and of Zeus and Themis at another.

```hql
import myth.kb
import myth.works
from myth.genealogy import accounts, disputed

works = kb.works()
births = kb.parentage()
name = (id: String) => names.get(id).unwrap_or(id)

disputed(births)
| flat_map(child => accounts(births, child) | map(a => {
    being: name(child),
    parents: a.parents | map(name) | join(" and "),
    source: works | find(w => w.id == a.work) | map(w => w.cite(a.passage)) | unwrap_or(a.work),
    note: a.note.unwrap_or(""),
  }))
| Table(columns = ["being", "parents", "source", "note"])
```
<!-- hql:output hash=b2c374f2f918 status=ok -->
| being       | parents         | source                              | note                                                                 |
| ----------- | --------------- | ----------------------------------- | -------------------------------------------------------------------- |
| Aphrodite   | Uranus          | Hesiod, Theogony 188–200            | from the foam around his severed genitals                            |
| Aphrodite   | Zeus and Dione  | Homer, Iliad 5.370–371              |                                                                      |
| Europa      | Phoenix         | Homer, Iliad 14.321                 |                                                                      |
| Europa      | Agenor          | Pseudo-Apollodorus, Library 3.1.1   | and Telephassa; some call her a daughter of Phoenix                  |
| Hephaestus  | Hera            | Hesiod, Theogony 927–929            | without union, in anger at Zeus                                      |
| Hephaestus  | Zeus and Hera   | Homer, Iliad 1.571–579              |                                                                      |
| Io          | Inachus         | Aeschylus, Prometheus Bound 589–590 |                                                                      |
| Io          | Iasus           | Pseudo-Apollodorus, Library 2.1.3   | Castor and many tragedians name Inachus; Hesiod and Acusilaus, Piren |
| Moirai      | Nyx             | Hesiod, Theogony 211–222            | without union                                                        |
| Moirai      | Zeus and Themis | Hesiod, Theogony 904–906            |                                                                      |
| Nemean Lion | Orthus          | Hesiod, Theogony 326–327            | with Chimaera or Echidna; the text does not say which                |
| Nemean Lion | Typhon          | Pseudo-Apollodorus, Library 2.5.1   |                                                                      |
